\title[The chain replacement of a poset flow]{The Chain Replacement of a Poset Flow:\\
Refinement Posets, Cones, and Order-Reflecting Inclusions}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop the combinatorial core of the \emph{chain replacement} of a poset flow. For a
poset $P$ and elements $x \le y$, let $\Ch(x,y)$ denote the set of finite, totally ordered
subsets of the interval $[x,y]$ containing both endpoints, ordered by refinement. The chain
replacement of the poset flow of $P$ is the flow whose states are the elements of $P$ and
whose space of execution paths from $x$ to $y$ is the simplicial nerve of $\Ch(x,y)$.

We prove: (i) $\Ch(x,y)$ is a cone, with least element the two-element chain $\{x,y\}$, and
the alternating face count of the order complex of any finite poset with a cone point
vanishes, by an explicit fixed-point-free sign-reversing involution; (ii) concatenation of
chains, given by union of carriers, is associative and monotone in each variable, making
$\Ch$ a semicategory enriched in posets, and it satisfies a unique factorisation property:
$\Ch(x,y) \times \Ch(y,z)$ is isomorphic as a poset to the sub-poset of chains of $\Ch(x,z)$
passing through $y$, with inverse given by cutting at $y$; (iii) the alternating chain count
equals $-\mu(x,y)$ (Philip Hall's theorem), whence $\mu(x,y)$ is the reduced Euler
characteristic of the order complex of the open interval $(x,y)$, and $\mu(x,y)=0$ whenever
$(x,y)$ has a cone point; (iv) for an order-reflecting inclusion $f : P \hookrightarrow Q$,
transport along $f$ and the trace along $f$ form a Galois coinsertion of refinement posets,
transport is an order embedding whose image is a lower set for refinement, its complement is
an upper set, and $\Ch_Q(fx,fy)$ splits as the disjoint union of the two --- the combinatorial
form of ``pushouts along the chain replacement of an order-reflecting inclusion preserve
spaces of execution paths''. An explicit two-element counterexample shows that
order-reflection cannot be weakened to injective monotonicity.
\end{abstract}

