\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Flows and directed spaces}

Directed algebraic topology studies spaces in which paths cannot be reversed, the motivating
example being the state space of a concurrent program: two independent atomic actions produce
a square of states in which every legal execution moves monotonically from the initial corner
to the final one. A \emph{flow} is one of the standard formalisations: a set of states, a
topological space $\mathbb{P}_{x,y}$ of execution paths for each ordered pair $(x,y)$ of
states, and associative concatenation maps
$\mathbb{P}_{x,y} \times \mathbb{P}_{y,z} \to \mathbb{P}_{x,z}$. Equivalently, a flow is a
semicategory (a category possibly without identities) enriched in topological spaces.

The simplest flows arise from partial orders. Given a poset $P$, the \emph{poset flow} of $P$
has state set $P$ and $\mathbb{P}_{x,y} = \ast$ if $x < y$ and $\varnothing$ otherwise, with
the unique possible composition; all information is carried by the order relation.

Homotopy theory of flows requires \emph{cofibrant replacements}: objects homotopy equivalent
to the given one but assembled by free attachments, so that constructions such as pushouts
compute the homotopically correct answer. Abstract machinery produces such replacements
opaquely. The chain replacement is the opposite: an explicit, finite, purely combinatorial
model.

\subsection{The chain replacement}

Fix a poset $P$ and $x \le y$. A \emph{chain from $x$ to $y$} is a finite subset
$C \subseteq P$ with $x,y \in C$, with $x \le a \le y$ for all $a \in C$, and with any two
elements of $C$ comparable; equivalently, a strictly increasing sequence
$x = t_0 < t_1 < \cdots < t_n = y$. Chains from $x$ to $y$ form the poset $\Ch(x,y)$ under
\emph{refinement}, $C \le D \iff C \subseteq D$. The \emph{chain replacement} of the poset
flow of $P$ is the flow with state set $P$ and $\mathbb{P}_{x,y} = \N(\Ch(x,y))$, the
simplicial nerve of the refinement poset, with concatenation induced by union of carriers.

Two features make this work. The nerve of a poset with a least element is contractible, and
$\Ch(x,y)$ has one, so nothing changes up to homotopy. And the refinement posets, together
with the inclusions between them, are combinatorially rigid enough that the cofibration and
gluing properties one wants can be verified by hand rather than invoked.

\subsection{Contributions}

Sections~\ref{sec:cone}--\ref{sec:reflect} prove, at the level of the refinement posets that
model the path spaces: the cone structure and an exact acyclicity statement; the
associativity, bimonotonicity and unique factorisation of concatenation; Philip Hall's
theorem, the Euler-characteristic reading of the M\"obius function and a vanishing criterion;
and, for order-reflecting inclusions, the transport/trace Galois coinsertion, the sieve
property, the splitting of path spaces and the necessity of order-reflection.
Section~\ref{sec:algo} gives algorithms and complexity, Section~\ref{sec:num} numerical
illustrations, and Sections~\ref{sec:disc}--\ref{sec:future} discussion and open problems.

\section{Definitions and conventions}

Throughout, $P$ and $Q$ denote partially ordered sets; finiteness is stated where needed. We
write $[x,y] = \{a : x \le a \le y\}$, $(x,y) = \{a : x < a < y\}$, and $|C|$ for the
cardinality of a finite set.

\begin{definition}[Chain from $x$ to $y$]
For $x,y \in P$, a \emph{chain from $x$ to $y$} is a finite subset $C \subseteq P$ such that
$x \in C$ and $y \in C$; $x \le a \le y$ for every $a \in C$; and any two elements of $C$ are
comparable. We write $\Ch(x,y)$ for the set of all such chains.
\end{definition}

Recording a chain by its underlying set rather than by an increasing sequence is deliberate:
refinement becomes literal inclusion, concatenation becomes literal union, and the two
segments of a chain cut at an interior point become literal filters.

\begin{definition}[Refinement order]
$\Ch(x,y)$ is partially ordered by $C \le D \iff C \subseteq D$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
$\Ch(x,y) \neq \varnothing$ if and only if $x \le y$: a chain contains $y$, whence $x \le y$
by boundedness; conversely $\{x,y\}$ is a chain when $x \le y$. If $P$ is finite then each
$\Ch(x,y)$ is finite.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[Order complex]
For a finite poset $R$, the \emph{order complex} $\Delta(R)$ is the abstract simplicial
complex whose faces are the totally ordered subsets of $R$, the empty set included. Its
realisation is the realisation of the nerve of $R$ viewed as a category.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cone point]
An element $z$ of a poset $R$ is a \emph{cone point} if $z \le a$ or $a \le z$ for every
$a \in R$. A least or greatest element is a cone point.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[M\"obius function]
For a finite poset $P$, the M\"obius function $\mu : P \times P \to \mathbb{Z}$ of the
incidence algebra is determined by $\mu(x,x)=1$, $\mu(x,y)=0$ if $x \not\le y$, and
$\mu(x,y) = -\sum_{x \le z < y}\mu(x,z)$ for $x<y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Order-reflecting inclusion]
A map $f : P \to Q$ is an \emph{order embedding}, equivalently an \emph{order-reflecting
inclusion}, if $u \le v \iff f(u) \le f(v)$ for all $u,v \in P$. Such an $f$ is automatically
injective; we write $f : P \hookrightarrow Q$.
\end{definition}

\section{The cone structure of the refinement poset}\label{sec:cone}

\begin{theorem}[Cone Theorem]\label{thm:cone}
Let $x \le y$ in a poset $P$. Then $\{x,y\}$ is a chain from $x$ to $y$ and is the least
element of $\Ch(x,y)$: $\{x,y\} \subseteq C$ for every $C \in \Ch(x,y)$. Consequently
$\Ch(x,y)$ has a bottom element and its nerve is contractible.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
That $\{x,y\}$ is a chain is immediate: it contains both endpoints; each of its elements lies
in $[x,y]$; and its two elements are comparable because $x \le y$. Minimality is the
definition of a chain from $x$ to $y$: every such chain contains $x$ and $y$. Contractibility
follows because the nerve of a poset with least element $\bot$ admits the contraction
determined by $C \mapsto C \cup \{\bot\}$, a natural transformation from the identity functor
to the constant functor at $\bot$.
\end{proof}

The numerical shadow of contractibility, proved in full below, is the version used in all
computations.

\begin{theorem}[Acyclicity of cones]\label{thm:acyclic}
Let $R$ be a finite poset possessing a cone point $z$. Then
\[
\sum_{C \in \Delta(R)} (-1)^{|C|} = 0,
\]
the sum ranging over all faces of the order complex, the empty face included. Equivalently
$\sum_{\varnothing \ne C \in \Delta(R)} (-1)^{|C|} = -1$, so that with the convention
$\dim C = |C|-1$ the Euler characteristic of $\Delta(R)$ is $1$ and its reduced Euler
characteristic is $0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define $\iota(C) = C \mathbin{\triangle} \{z\}$: delete $z$ if present, insert it otherwise.
Then $\iota$ maps faces to faces (removing an element from a totally ordered set leaves it
totally ordered; adjoining $z$ preserves total ordering precisely because $z$ is comparable
with every element of $R$); $\iota$ is an involution without fixed points, since
$z \in C \iff z \notin \iota(C)$ and $\iota(\iota(C)) = C$; and $\iota$ reverses signs, since
$|\iota(C)| = |C| \pm 1$. A fixed-point-free sign-reversing involution on a finite index set
forces the sum to vanish. The reduced form follows by isolating the empty face, of
contribution $(-1)^0 = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:chainacyclic}
For $x \le y$ in a finite poset $P$,
\[
\sum_{\mathcal{C} \in \Delta(\Ch(x,y))} (-1)^{|\mathcal{C}|} = 0,
\qquad
\sum_{\varnothing \ne \mathcal{C} \in \Delta(\Ch(x,y))} (-1)^{|\mathcal{C}|} = -1 .
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:acyclic} to $R = \Ch(x,y)$ with cone point $\{x,y\}$, which is
comparable with everything because it is below everything (Theorem~\ref{thm:cone}).
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:chainacyclic} says the path spaces of the chain replacement have the Euler
characteristic of a point. The hypothesis is sharp: for the two-element antichain, the order
complex consists of $\varnothing$ and two vertices, with alternating face count
$1-1-1 = -1 \ne 0$, recording two contractible components.

\section{Composition and unique factorisation}\label{sec:comp}

\begin{definition}
For $C \in \Ch(x,y)$ and $D \in \Ch(y,z)$, set $C \ast D := C \cup D$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:concat}
$C \ast D \in \Ch(x,z)$, and $y \in C \ast D$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$x \in C$ and $z \in D$ give the endpoints. Boundedness: if $a \in C$ then $x \le a$ and
$a \le y \le z$; symmetrically for $a \in D$. Total ordering: two elements of $C$, or two of
$D$, are comparable by hypothesis; and if $a \in C$, $b \in D$ then $a \le y \le b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Poset-enriched semicategory]\label{thm:semicat}
Concatenation is associative,
$(C \ast D) \ast E = C \ast (D \ast E)$ for $C \in \Ch(x,y)$, $D \in \Ch(y,z)$,
$E \in \Ch(z,w)$, and monotone in each variable: $C \le C'$ implies
$C \ast D \le C' \ast D$, and $D \le D'$ implies $C \ast D \le C \ast D'$. Hence $\Ch$ is a
semicategory enriched in posets and, after applying the nerve, a flow.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Associativity is associativity of union; monotonicity is monotonicity of union in each
argument. Bimonotone maps of posets induce simplicial maps of nerves, realising concatenation
as a map $\N(\Ch(x,y)) \times \N(\Ch(y,z)) \to \N(\Ch(x,z))$.
\end{proof}

Note that $\{x,y\} \ast \{y,z\} = \{x,y,z\}$: the composite of the two coarsest chains is the
coarsest chain \emph{through} $y$, not the coarsest chain from $x$ to $z$.

\begin{definition}
For $E \in \Ch(x,z)$ with $y \in E$, put $E_{\le y} := \{a \in E : a \le y\}$ and
$E_{\ge y} := \{a \in E : y \le a\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cut}
$E_{\le y} \in \Ch(x,y)$ and $E_{\ge y} \in \Ch(y,z)$, and both operations are monotone in
$E$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$x \in E_{\le y}$ because $x \le y$, and $y \in E_{\le y}$; boundedness and total ordering are
inherited from $E$; likewise on the other side. Filtering both sides of an inclusion by the
same predicate preserves it.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Cut--glue inverse laws]\label{lem:cutglue}
For $E \in \Ch(x,z)$ with $y \in E$ we have $E_{\le y} \ast E_{\ge y} = E$. Conversely, for
$C \in \Ch(x,y)$ and $D \in \Ch(y,z)$ we have $(C \ast D)_{\le y} = C$ and
$(C \ast D)_{\ge y} = D$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For the first identity, $\subseteq$ is clear; for $\supseteq$, take $a \in E$: since $E$ is
totally ordered and $y \in E$, either $a \le y$ or $y \le a$. For the second, $\supseteq$
holds since every $a \in C$ satisfies $a \le y$; for $\subseteq$, if $a \in C \cup D$ and
$a \le y$ then either $a \in C$, or $a \in D$ and then $y \le a$, so $a = y \in C$. The
right-hand identity is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique Factorisation Theorem]\label{thm:factor}
For $x,y,z$ in a poset $P$, the map
\[
\Phi : \Ch(x,y) \times \Ch(y,z) \longrightarrow \{E \in \Ch(x,z) : y \in E\},
\qquad \Phi(C,D) = C \ast D,
\]
is an isomorphism of posets, the left-hand side carrying the product order and the right-hand
side the refinement order, with inverse $E \mapsto (E_{\le y}, E_{\ge y})$. Moreover $\Phi$
reflects the order: $C \ast D \subseteq C' \ast D'$ if and only if $C \subseteq C'$ and
$D \subseteq D'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Well-definedness is Proposition~\ref{prop:concat}, and the two composites are the identity by
Lemma~\ref{lem:cutglue}. Monotonicity of $\Phi$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:semicat} and of its
inverse is Lemma~\ref{lem:cut}. Order reflection follows: apply the monotone cutting maps to
$C \ast D \subseteq C' \ast D'$ and use Lemma~\ref{lem:cutglue}.
\end{proof}

Under the nerve, Theorem~\ref{thm:factor} states that concatenation identifies
$\mathbb{P}_{x,y} \times \mathbb{P}_{y,z}$ with the full simplicial subset of
$\mathbb{P}_{x,z}$ spanned by the executions that stop at $y$ --- an on-the-nose statement,
strictly stronger than its homotopical counterpart.

\section{Alternating chain counts and the M\"obius function}\label{sec:hall}

Throughout this section $P$ is finite.

\begin{definition}
For $x,y \in P$ put $\chi(x,y) := \sum_{C \in \Ch(x,y)} (-1)^{|C|} \in \mathbb{Z}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Boundary values]\label{lem:bdry}
$\chi(x,x) = -1$, and $\chi(x,y) = 0$ when $x \not\le y$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Every chain from $x$ to $x$ is contained in $[x,x] = \{x\}$ and contains $x$, hence equals
$\{x\}$: the sum is $(-1)^1 = -1$. If $x \not\le y$ there is no chain at all, and the empty
sum is $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Decapitation recursion]\label{lem:decap}
For $x < y$ we have $\chi(x,y) = -\sum_{z \in [x,y)} \chi(x,z)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Every nonempty finite totally ordered set has a greatest element; for a chain $C$ from $x$ to
$y$ that element is $y$. The assignment $C \mapsto (z, C \setminus \{y\})$ with
$z = \max(C \setminus \{y\})$ is a bijection
$\Ch(x,y) \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ } \coprod_{z \in [x,y)} \Ch(x,z)$, with inverse
$(z,C') \mapsto C' \cup \{y\}$: indeed $C \setminus \{y\}$ is nonempty (it contains $x$),
totally ordered, bounded above by its maximum $z < y$, and contains $x$ and $z$; conversely
adjoining $y$ to a chain from $x$ to $z$ with $z<y$ produces a chain from $x$ to $y$ whose
second-largest element is $z$. Since $|C' \cup \{y\}| = |C'|+1$, each sign is multiplied by
$-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Philip Hall's Theorem]\label{thm:hall}
For all $x,y$ in a finite poset $P$,
\[
\sum_{C \in \Ch(x,y)} (-1)^{|C|} = -\mu(x,y).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $y$ with respect to the well-founded strict order. If $x = y$, both sides equal
$-1$. If $x \not\le y$, both sides are $0$. If $x<y$, Lemma~\ref{lem:decap} and the inductive
hypothesis give
$\chi(x,y) = -\sum_{z \in [x,y)}\chi(x,z) = \sum_{z\in[x,y)}\mu(x,z) = -\mu(x,y)$, the last
step being the defining recursion of $\mu$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\mu(x,y) = 0$ whenever $x \not\le y$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[M\"obius function as reduced Euler characteristic]\label{thm:mueuler}
For $x<y$ in a finite poset $P$,
\[
\sum_{F \in \Delta((x,y))} (-1)^{|F|} = -\mu(x,y),
\]
the sum ranging over the faces of the order complex of the open interval $(x,y)$, the empty
face included. Equivalently, $\mu(x,y)$ is the reduced Euler characteristic of
$\Delta((x,y))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The map $F \mapsto F \cup \{x,y\}$ is a bijection from the faces of $\Delta((x,y))$ to
$\Ch(x,y)$: it is well defined because $x$ and $y$ are comparable with every element of
$(x,y)$; injective because $x,y \notin (x,y)$, so $F = C \setminus \{x,y\}$; and surjective
because $C \cap (x,y)$ is a face mapping to $C$. As $|F \cup \{x,y\}| = |F|+2$, the signs
agree; conclude with Theorem~\ref{thm:hall}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing criterion]\label{thm:vanish}
Let $x<y$ in a finite poset $P$. If the open interval $(x,y)$ contains an element $z$
comparable with every element of $(x,y)$, then $\mu(x,y)=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:acyclic} applied to $R = (x,y)$ with cone point $z$, the alternating face
count of $\Delta((x,y))$ vanishes; by Theorem~\ref{thm:mueuler} that count is $-\mu(x,y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The criterion is sufficient but not necessary: vanishing reduced Euler characteristic is much
weaker than contractibility, so any interval whose order complex is, for instance, an odd
homology sphere yields $\mu = 0$ without a cone point.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:vanish} applies verbatim to refinement posets, whose cone point is
$\{x,y\}$; it is the same computation as Corollary~\ref{cor:chainacyclic}, viewed through the
incidence algebra rather than the order complex. This is the precise sense in which the
homotopical content of the chain replacement and the enumerative content of Hall's theorem
are a single statement.
\end{remark}

\section{Order-reflecting inclusions, cofibrations, and gluing}\label{sec:reflect}

Let $f : P \hookrightarrow Q$ be an order embedding, and fix $x,y \in P$. Write $\Ch_P$,
$\Ch_Q$ for refinement posets computed in $P$ and in $Q$.

\begin{definition}[Transport]
For $C \in \Ch_P(x,y)$, set $f_\ast C := f(C)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$f_\ast C \in \Ch_Q(fx,fy)$, and $f_\ast$ is monotone.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$f(x), f(y) \in f(C)$; boundedness and comparability are preserved because $f$ is
order-preserving; monotonicity is monotonicity of images.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Trace]
For $E \in \Ch_Q(fx,fy)$, set $f^\ast E := f^{-1}(E) = \{a \in P : f(a) \in E\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:trace}
$f^\ast E \in \Ch_P(x,y)$, and $f^\ast$ is monotone. This is the only place where
order-reflection is used, and it is indispensable.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$x \in f^\ast E$ since $f(x) \in E$, similarly $y$. If $a \in f^\ast E$ then
$f(x) \le f(a) \le f(y)$, and order-reflection gives $x \le a \le y$. If $a,b \in f^\ast E$
then $f(a), f(b)$ are comparable in $E$, and reflection converts this to comparability of $a$
and $b$. Monotonicity is monotonicity of preimages. Without reflection the last two steps
fail: the preimage of a chain can be an antichain.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Galois coinsertion]\label{thm:galois}
For every $C \in \Ch_P(x,y)$ and $E \in \Ch_Q(fx,fy)$,
\[
f_\ast C \le E \iff C \le f^\ast E, \qquad\text{and}\qquad f^\ast f_\ast C = C .
\]
Thus $(f_\ast, f^\ast)$ is a monotone Galois connection with $f^\ast f_\ast = \mathrm{id}$,
i.e.\ a Galois coinsertion.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$f(C) \subseteq E$ iff every $a \in C$ has $f(a) \in E$, iff $C \subseteq f^{-1}(E)$. And
$f^{-1}(f(C)) = C$ because $f$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$f_\ast$ is an order embedding $\Ch_P(x,y) \hookrightarrow \Ch_Q(fx,fy)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Injectivity from $f^\ast f_\ast = \mathrm{id}$. If $f_\ast C \le f_\ast D$ then
$C \le f^\ast f_\ast D = D$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:galois}; the converse is monotonicity.
\end{proof}

An adjunction between posets induces a homotopy equivalence of nerves, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:galois} is the structural reason the induced map of path spaces is so well
behaved.

\begin{proposition}[Compatibility with the flow structure]
For $C \in \Ch_P(x,y)$ and $D \in \Ch_P(y,z)$ one has
$f_\ast(C \ast D) = f_\ast C \ast f_\ast D$ and $f_\ast \{x,y\} = \{fx,fy\}$, and
$(g \circ f)_\ast = g_\ast \circ f_\ast$ for a further order embedding
$g : Q \hookrightarrow R$. Hence the chain replacement is functorial and $f_\ast$ is a
morphism of flows.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Images commute with unions and with the two-element set; functoriality of images.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Recognition]\label{lem:recog}
$E \in \Ch_Q(fx,fy)$ is in the image of $f_\ast$ if and only if $E \subseteq f(P)$, if and
only if $f_\ast f^\ast E = E$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $E = f_\ast C$ then $E \subseteq f(P)$. Conversely, if $E \subseteq f(P)$ then
$f(f^{-1}(E)) = E$, and $f^\ast E$ is a chain by Proposition~\ref{prop:trace}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sieve property]\label{thm:sieve}
The set $\mathcal{L} := \{E \in \Ch_Q(fx,fy) : \exists\, C,\ f_\ast C = E\}$ is a lower set
for refinement; dually, its complement is an upper set.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $E = f_\ast C$ and $E' \subseteq E$. Every element of $E'$ lies in $E \subseteq f(P)$, so
$E'$ is supported on $f(P)$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:recog} applies.
\end{proof}

Lower sets for refinement are precisely the sieves --- subobjects closed under coarsening ---
and this closure property is what makes the induced inclusion of nerves a cofibration: the
attaching happens along a full, downward-closed sub-poset, so the cells of the complement are
attached freely.

\begin{theorem}[Splitting]\label{thm:split}
There is a bijection
\[
\Ch_P(x,y) \;\sqcup\; \{E \in \Ch_Q(fx,fy) : E \notin \mathcal{L}\}
\;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; \Ch_Q(fx,fy),
\]
given by $f_\ast$ on the first summand and the inclusion on the second, with inverse
$E \mapsto f^\ast E$ when $E \in \mathcal{L}$ and $E \mapsto E$ otherwise. The image of the
first summand is a lower set and the second summand is an upper set for refinement.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The two summands are complementary by Lemma~\ref{lem:recog}, and $f_\ast$ is a bijection onto
$\mathcal{L}$ with inverse $f^\ast$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:galois} and Lemma~\ref{lem:recog}. The
lower/upper set statements are Theorem~\ref{thm:sieve}.
\end{proof}

\subsection*{Interpretation}
Attaching new material to the chain replacement of $P$ along $f$ forms a pushout of flows.
Theorem~\ref{thm:split} says that as a poset --- hence, after taking nerves, as a space ---
each path space of the chain replacement of $Q$ is the disjoint union of a copy of the
corresponding path space of the chain replacement of $P$, sitting as a downward-closed piece,
and an independent remainder. Replacing the first piece leaves the remainder untouched, so the
path space of the pushout is the pushout of the path spaces: pushouts along the chain
replacement of an order-reflecting inclusion of finite posets preserve spaces of execution
paths.

\begin{theorem}[Necessity of order-reflection]\label{thm:necessary}
Let $P$ be the two-element antichain $\{a,b\}$ and $Q$ the two-element chain $\{0<1\}$, and
let $g : P \to Q$ be $g(a)=0$, $g(b)=1$. Then $g$ is injective and monotone but not
order-reflecting; the chain $\{0,1\} \in \Ch_Q(ga,gb)$ is supported on the image of $g$; and
$\Ch_P(a,b) = \varnothing$. Hence Lemma~\ref{lem:recog}, and with it
Theorems~\ref{thm:sieve} and~\ref{thm:split}, fails for merely injective monotone maps.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Monotonicity: $u \le v$ in $P$ means $u=v$, so $g(u)=g(v)$; injectivity is clear; reflection
fails since $g(a)=0 \le 1 = g(b)$ while $a \not\le b$. The set $\{0,1\}$ is a chain from $0$
to $1$ in $Q$ with both elements in the image of $g$. A chain from $a$ to $b$ in $P$ would
force $a \le b$, which is false.
\end{proof}

The failure is maximal: the source path space is empty while the target path space is a
point. The trace itself breaks, since $g^{-1}(\{0,1\}) = \{a,b\}$ is an antichain.

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algo}

Let $P$ be a finite poset presented by its comparability matrix, $n = |P|$.

\subsection*{Enumeration of chains}
Chains from $x$ to $y$ correspond to totally ordered subsets of $(x,y)$. Enumerate by
depth-first search on the last stop: starting at $x$, extend the current chain by any $t$ with
$\mathrm{last} < t \le y$, emitting when $t=y$. Each chain is produced exactly once because
the sequence of stops is strictly increasing; the cost is $O(n)$ per emitted chain, hence
$O(n \cdot |\Ch(x,y)|)$ overall. The count alone is obtainable in $O(n^3)$ from the unsigned
recursion $N(x,y) = \sum_{x \le t < y} N(x,t)$, $N(x,x)=1$.

\subsection*{M\"obius function, two ways}
Either by the classical recursion $\mu(x,y) = -\sum_{x \le z < y}\mu(x,z)$, computable in
$O(n^3)$ for all pairs by processing $y$ along a linear extension; or as the alternating chain
count $-\sum_C (-1)^{|C|}$, exponential in general but structurally informative.
Theorem~\ref{thm:hall} asserts these agree, which is a stringent consistency test.

\subsection*{Refinement poset and splitting test}
Enumerate $\Ch(x,y)$ and order the results by inclusion to obtain the refinement poset with
$\{x,y\}$ as bottom. For an order embedding $f$, classify each $E \in \Ch_Q(fx,fy)$ by the
predicate $E \subseteq f(P)$: Theorem~\ref{thm:split} predicts that the ``yes'' class is in
bijection with $\Ch_P(x,y)$ via $f^\ast$ and is closed downwards, while the ``no'' class is
closed upwards. Both checks are quadratic in the number of chains.

\section{Numerical illustrations}\label{sec:num}

\textbf{The chain $0<1<2<3$.} Chains from $0$ to $3$ are determined by the subset of
$\{1,2\}$ used as stopovers: there are $4$, of sizes $2,3,3,4$, with alternating sum
$1-1-1+1=0 = -\mu(0,3)$. For the covering pair $0<1$ there is one chain, alternating sum $1 =
-\mu(0,1)$.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{The Boolean lattice $B_n$.} A chain from $\varnothing$ to $\{1,\dots,n\}$ is
an ordered partition of $\{1,\dots,n\}$ into nonempty blocks, so the counts are the ordered
Bell (Fubini) numbers $1, 3, 13, 75, \dots$. For $n=3$ the thirteen chains give alternating
sum $1 = -\mu(\varnothing,\top)$, consistent with $\mu_{B_n}(\varnothing,\top) = (-1)^n$. The
open interval $(\varnothing,\{1,2\})$ inside $B_3$ is a two-element antichain, with alternating
face count $-1 = -\mu$ and $\mu = 1 = (-1)^2$.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Cone points.} The alternating face count of the order complex is $0$ for the
four-element chain and for $B_2$ (both have least elements) and $-1$ for the two-element
antichain --- the sharpness of Theorem~\ref{thm:acyclic}.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Refinement posets and splitting.} For $B_3$ with $x=\varnothing$, $y=\top$,
the refinement poset has $13$ elements, unique bottom $\{\varnothing,\top\}$ and six maximal
elements, and alternating face count $0$ as Corollary~\ref{cor:chainacyclic} predicts. Taking
$Q = B_2$ and $P = \{\varnothing, \{1\}, \top\}$ order-reflectingly included, of the three
chains of $\Ch_Q(\varnothing,\top)$ exactly two are supported on $P$ and form a lower set,
matching $|\Ch_P| = 2$, while $\{\varnothing,\{2\},\top\}$ is the upper-set remainder.

\section{Applications and discussion}\label{sec:disc}

\textbf{Directed topology and concurrency.} The chain replacement gives an explicit cofibrant
model for the flows arising from precedence orders on concurrent systems; being finite and
combinatorial, questions about path spaces reduce to questions about refinement posets. The
splitting theorem says precisely when adding behaviour along a subsystem cannot disturb the
execution spaces of the ambient system: the subsystem must be embedded order-reflectingly,
i.e.\ the ambient system must not create precedence between actions the subsystem regards as
independent. Theorem~\ref{thm:necessary} is the smallest counterexample and has an operational
reading: if the enclosing system serialises two actions the subsystem considers concurrent,
the subsystem's execution space is not recoverable from the ambient one.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Enumerative combinatorics.} Theorem~\ref{thm:hall} is Philip Hall's classical
theorem, here derived from the same decapitation bijection that structures the refinement
poset; Theorem~\ref{thm:mueuler} places $\mu$ in topology; Theorem~\ref{thm:vanish} yields an
easily applied criterion: intervals with a universally comparable element contribute nothing
to M\"obius inversion.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Rigidity.} Theorem~\ref{thm:factor} is an isomorphism of posets, not an
equivalence up to homotopy, and Theorem~\ref{thm:split} is a bijection, not a weak equivalence.
The chain replacement achieves the flexibility of a cofibrant replacement without giving up
strictness at the combinatorial level.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{problem}[Nerve contractibility upgrade]
For every finite poset $R$ with a cone point, the simplicial nerve of $R$ is a contractible
simplicial set; consequently, for $x \le y$ in a finite poset, the nerve of $\Ch(x,y)$ is
contractible and the chain replacement of a poset flow is a genuine cofibrant replacement.
\end{problem}

The involution $C \mapsto C \mathbin{\triangle} \{z\}$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:acyclic} is the
shadow of an explicit simplicial homotopy $\Delta^1 \times \N(R) \to \N(R)$ contracting
$\N(R)$ onto the cone point; the numerical statement proved here is the Euler characteristic of
a homotopy that can be written down face by face. The claim is falsifiable: a finite poset with
a cone point whose nerve has a nontrivial homotopy group would refute it.

\begin{problem}[Fibrewise product formula for the trace]
Let $f : P \hookrightarrow Q$ be order-reflecting and $x,y \in P$. For every
$D \in \Ch_P(x,y)$ the fibre $\{E \in \Ch_Q(fx,fy) : f^\ast E = D\}$ is isomorphic, as a poset,
to the product over the covering pairs $(u,v)$ of $D$ of the posets of chains from $f(u)$ to
$f(v)$ whose interior misses the image of $P$.
\end{problem}

The Galois coinsertion already splits the chain poset of $Q$ into a lower set and its
complement, and the two-step version of the splitting is exactly Theorem~\ref{thm:factor}; the
general statement should follow by induction on $|D|$, using that isomorphism at each cut
point. The missing ingredient is an indexing scheme for covering pairs of a finite chain. A
finite order-reflecting inclusion and a chain $D$ whose fibre has cardinality different from
the product would refute it.

\begin{problem}[M\"obius vanishing from local cones is not exhaustive]
The cone criterion of Theorem~\ref{thm:vanish} is strictly sufficient: there exist finite
posets and pairs $x<y$ with $\mu(x,y)=0$ for which $(x,y)$ has no cone point, and the class of
such accidentally vanishing intervals is not captured by any finite list of local comparability
criteria.
\end{problem}

The reason to expect this is that $\mu(x,y)$ records only the reduced Euler characteristic of
$\Delta((x,y))$, whereas a cone point records contractibility; every complex with vanishing
reduced Euler characteristic and nontrivial homology yields a candidate.

Beyond these: a \emph{relative} chain replacement, identifying the relative path spaces of a
pair $(Q,P)$ with the upper-set remainder of Theorem~\ref{thm:split} and thus giving a
combinatorial model for the cofibre; and a quantitative study of the size of $\Ch(x,y)$ as a
function of the width and height of the poset, to gauge how practical the model is for the
state spaces arising from actual concurrent programs.

\section{Conclusion}

The chain replacement rests on one observation: the chains from $x$ to $y$, ordered by
refinement, form a poset with a least element. From it flow the contractibility of the replaced
path spaces, an exact acyclicity statement proved by a fixed-point-free sign-reversing
involution, a strict unique-factorisation law for concatenation, Philip Hall's theorem and the
identification of the M\"obius function with a reduced Euler characteristic, and --- for
order-reflecting inclusions --- a Galois coinsertion, a sieve property, and a clean splitting
of path spaces that makes gluing behave. The two-element counterexample shows that
order-reflection is not a technical convenience but the exact hypothesis under which the theory
holds.

